SED(1)                         P32W Unix                          SED(1)

NAME
       sed - the stream editor

SYNOPSIS
       sed [-nEs] [-i[suffix]] script [file ...]
       sed [-nEs] [-i[suffix]] [-e script] ... [-f file] ... [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       sed reads each file in turn (the standard input if none is
       named, or for a file named -), a line at a time, runs the
       script on each line and writes the result to the standard
       output. The files are one stream: line numbers run on from
       file to file and $ is the last line of the last file.

       Each line is read into the pattern space, without its
       newline. The commands of the script are applied to it in
       order, and at the end of the script the pattern space is
       written out (unless -n is given) and the next line is read.
       A second buffer, the hold space, keeps text between lines.
       Lines may be of any length. A last line without a newline is
       written without one.

       The script is made of commands, one per line or separated by
       ;. Blanks before a command and # comments are ignored. If
       the script starts with the line #n, it is as if -n were
       given.

OPTIONS
       Options must come before the script and the files.

       -n     do not write the pattern space at the end of the script
              (only p, P, l, = and the like write)
       -e script
              add script to the script; several -e may be given, each
              ends a line
       -f file
              add the commands in file to the script
       -E, -r use extended regular expressions (below)
       -s     treat the files as separate: line numbers start at 1
              and $ is the last line of each file
       -i[suffix]
              edit each file in place (implies -s): the output goes
              to a new file that replaces it; with a suffix (-i.bak)
              the old file is kept as file.bak

ADDRESSES
       A command may have no address (every line), one address (the
       lines it matches) or two, addr1,addr2: a range from a line
       that matches addr1 through the next line that matches addr2
       (searched from the line after addr1; when addr2 is a number
       no greater than addr1's line, the range is that one line).
       A ! after the addresses selects the lines not matched.

       N      line N (0,/re/ is a range that may end on line 1)
       $      the last line
       /re/   lines that match the regular expression re
       \cREc  the same, with c in place of / as the delimiter
       /re/I  the same, ignoring case
       F~S    every S-th line from line F (0~2: the even lines)
       addr1,+N
              addr1 and the N lines after it
       addr1,~N
              addr1 through the next line whose number is a multiple
              of N

COMMANDS
       Commands marked * take at most one address; : and } none.

       {      the commands up to the matching } run only on the
              selected lines (blocks may nest)
       s/re/rep/flags
              substitute rep for the first match of re (see below)
       y/abc/xyz/
              change each a to x, b to y, c to z; the strings must
              be the same length (\n is a newline, \\ a backslash)
       p      write the pattern space
       P      write the pattern space up to its first newline
       d      delete the pattern space and start the next cycle
       D      if the pattern space holds no newline, as d; otherwise
              delete up to and including the first newline and start
              the next cycle without reading a line
       n      write the pattern space (not with -n) and replace it
              with the next line; with no next line, end as at the
              end of the script
       N      add a newline and the next line to the pattern space;
              with no next line, end as at the end of the script
       h, H   copy (h) or add a newline and (H) the pattern space to
              the hold space
       g, G   copy (g) or add a newline and (G) the hold space to the
              pattern space
       x      exchange the pattern and hold spaces
       z      empty the pattern space
       a\     text: write text at the end of the cycle (or when n
              or N reads the next line). The text is the next line;
              a line ending in \ continues it, and a \ before any
              other character is removed. a text (on one line) is
              the same. i\ and c\ take text in the same way
       i\     text: write text now
       c\     text: delete the pattern space and write text; with a
              range, the text is written at the range's end
       =      write the line number
       l [n]  write the pattern space unambiguously: \\ \a \b \f \n
              \r \t \v, other unprintable characters as \ooo, long
              lines folded at n characters (70; 0 never) with \, and
              $ at the end
       r file write the file at the end of the cycle (nothing if it
              cannot be read)
       w file write the pattern space to file (created when sed
              starts; /dev/stdout and /dev/stderr are understood)
       b [label]
              branch to label, or to the end of the script
       t [label]
              branch if a substitution was made since the last line
              was read or the last t
       T [label]
              branch if none was made
       :label a label for b, t and T
       q [code] *
              end: write the pattern space (not with -n) and any
              appended text, and exit with code (0)
       Q [code] *
              exit at once, writing nothing

       The file names of r and w, and the text of a, i and c, run to
       the end of the line; a label ends at ; or the end of the line.

SUBSTITUTION
       In s the delimiter may be any character but \ and newline; \
       before it makes it an ordinary character. In the replacement
       rep, & is the matched text, \1 to \9 the text matched by the
       groups \( \) (or ( ) with -E), \n a newline, \t a tab, \& and
       \\ the characters, and \ before a newline a newline. \U and
       \L turn the rest into capitals or small letters (up to \E),
       \u and \l the next character only. The flags:

       g      replace every match, not only the first
       N      replace the N-th match only (Ng: the N-th and after)
       p      write the pattern space if a substitution was made
       w file write it to file, likewise (last flag: file runs to
              the end of the line)
       I, i   ignore case

       An empty match next to the previous match is not replaced, so
       s/x*/-/g turns abc into -a-b-c-. An empty regular expression
       (as in s//X/ or //) is the last one used.

REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
       Basic regular expressions (the default):

       c      an ordinary character matches itself
       \c     c literally, for the special characters . [ \ * ^ $
       .      any character (also a newline in the pattern space)
       [...]  a character in the set: ranges a-z, classes such as
              [:alpha:] [:digit:] [:alnum:] [:upper:] [:lower:]
              [:space:] [:blank:] [:punct:] [:print:] [:graph:]
              [:cntrl:] [:xdigit:]; ] first is a member, - first or
              last is a member; \n is a newline
       [^...] a character not in the set
       ^ $    the beginning, the end of the pattern space (^ only at
              the start of the expression or a group, $ only at the
              end; elsewhere they are ordinary)
       *      zero or more of the preceding item (first: ordinary)
       \{m,n\}
              m to n of the preceding item; \{m\} exactly m, \{m,\}
              at least m
       \( \)  a group, for \1 to \9
       \1-\9  the text the group matched
       \+ \?  one or more, zero or one of the preceding item
       \|     either side (alternation)
       \n \t  newline, tab
       \w \W  a word character (letter, digit, _), and any other
       \s \S  a space character, and any other
       \< \> \b \B
              the start, the end of a word, a word boundary, not one

       Extended regular expressions (-E) are the same but for:
       + ? | ( ) { } are special without a backslash, and \+ \( and
       the like are the ordinary characters; ^ and $ are anchors
       everywhere.

       The match found is the leftmost, and of those the longest
       (a|ab matches all of ab).

EXAMPLES
       sed -n 10,20p file           lines 10 to 20
       sed '/^#/d;/^$/d' rc         without comments or blank lines
       sed 's/[[:space:]]*$//'      strip trailing blanks
       sed -n '$='                  count lines
       sed '1!G;h;$!d'              the lines in reverse order
       sed ':a;N;$!ba;s/\n/,/g'     join all the lines with commas
       sed '$!N;P;D'                (a sliding window of two lines)
       sed -E 's/([^ ]+) ([^ ]+)/\2 \1/' swap the first two words
       sed -i.bak 's/old/new/g' *.c edit files in place, keep backups

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error or an error in the script; 2 if an
       input file could not be read (sed says so and goes on); 4 for
       an output error; or the code given to q or Q.

SEE ALSO
       ed(1), grep(1), vi(1), sh(1)

BUGS
       Options must come before the script and the files. There are
       no --long options and no -z, -u, -l, e, F, R, W, M or v. With
       back-references in a pattern, the first match found
       (preferring longer repetitions and the left alternative) is
       used rather than the longest. The groups of an alternation
       report the first way the longest match was found, which may
       differ from POSIX's rule for subexpressions. Repetition
       counts are at most 255.
